using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

// Refactor this later to be less brute forced
public class StoryData : MonoBehaviour
{
    public static statueStage currentStatueStage;
    public static bookStage currentBookStage;
    public static string currentGameMode;
    private static string storyString;


    public static void SetCurrentHighOrderStage(statueStage stage)
    {
        // conditionals for each stage to be implemented later
        // if (stage == statueStage.HO_1)
        // {
        //     storyString = "Once upon a time, in a faraway land, there was a kingdom called the Kingdom of Hearts. The kingdom was ruled by a kind and just king, who was loved by all his subjects. The king had a beautiful daughter, Princess Aurora, who was known for her kindness and beauty. The princess was loved by all the people of the kingdom, and many princes from neighboring kingdoms came to seek her hand in marriage. However, the princess was not interested in any of them, as she had not yet found her true love.";
        // }
        currentGameMode = "HighOrder";
        currentStatueStage = stage;

        if (currentStatueStage == statueStage.HO_1)
        {
            storyString = "The Story of the Moth \n\nOne night, the whole family had gone to bed early—except for my mother and me. I don’t know why, but the two of us stayed up, sitting quietly together. \n\nThe candles were already put out using a curved tin blower. The room was dim, lit only by a coconut oil lamp.\n\nThat night, my mother was teaching me how to read a book called El Amigo de los Niños. She grew impatient because I was reading poorly. She also scolded me for drawing silly pictures in the book. \n\nThen she said, “Just listen,” and began to read aloud herself. \n\nI got tired of listening to words I didn’t understand. I turned my eyes to the little flame instead. It danced cheerfully. Some small moths were flying around it in circles. \n\nI happened to yawn. My mother saw that I was losing interest, so she stopped reading. \n\n“I’ll read you a very nice story now,” she said. \n\n“You must listen.” \n\nI opened my eyes wide. This sounded new and exciting. She began reading a fable about an old moth and a young moth, translating it into Tagalog as she read.\n\nFrom the very first sentence, I was interested. I stared at the lamp and watched the moths flying around it.\n\nThe story was like this: \n\nA mother moth once warned her daughter not to go near a flame. The light may look beautiful and inviting, but it was dangerous. It could burn anyone who got too close. \n\nThe mother moth said she herself had once flown too near the flame and barely escaped. She had half-burned her wings. \n\nThe young moth promised to obey. But soon, she began to wonder, \n\n“Why is my mother trying to scare me? Why should I close my eyes to such pretty light?” \n\n“Old people are always afraid of everything! What harm can it do me if I go near—just carefully? I’ll have such a good story to tell if I look closer.”\n\nSo she flew around the flame. At first, it felt pleasantly warm. That gave her more courage, so she flew closer and closer. \n\nBut then she got too near. Dazzled by the light, she fell into the flame and died.\n\nAs my mother tucked me into bed, she said:\n\n“Don’t be like the young moth. Don’t disobey, or you may get hurt the same way.”\n\nI don’t remember if I answered her. But the story opened my eyes.\n\nI never saw moths the same way again. They were no longer just small insects. Now I believed they could talk. They could warn and give advice—just like my mother.\n\nThe flame also looked different to me. It seemed more beautiful. It glowed brighter and felt more magical. I finally understood why moths flew around the light.\n\n– THE END –";
        }
        else if (currentStatueStage == statueStage.HO_2)
        {
            storyString = "The Turtle and The Rabbit\n\nOne afternoon, the Turtle and the Rabbit met on the road. The Rabbit teased the Turtle about his small feet, saying that was why he walked so slowly.\n\nThe Turtle felt insulted by the Rabbit’s jokes. Wanting to prove the Rabbit wrong, he challenged him and said,\n\n“If you really want to test my ability, why don’t we settle it with a race? You may be fast, but I have strong endurance.”\n\n“What kind of race do you have in mind?” asked the Rabbit.\n\n“If you’re willing, let’s race to the top of the third mountain,” answered the Turtle. \n\nThe Rabbit was delighted by the challenge. He was confident he would win. They each invited their friends to watch the competition.\n\nThe next morning, many animals gathered to spectate. \n\nThe carabao that had once raced the Turtle was there, along with a horse, pig, frog, duck, chicken, dog, cat, goat, cow, hawk, deer, wild boar, and others. \n\nEveryone was excited—it was the first time they’d witness such a race!\n\nA monkey gave the signal to start.\n\nFrom the beginning, the Turtle was left far behind. He moved so slowly that the Rabbit could barely see him.\n\nWhen the Rabbit reached the top of the second mountain, he still saw no sign of the Turtle behind him.\n\n“Ah, I might as well rest. The Turtle is still far behind,” the Rabbit said to himself.\n\nHe sat under a tree and, exhausted, fell asleep without realizing it.\n\nMeanwhile, the Turtle reached the top of the second mountain just before noon. He found the Rabbit snoring loudly—likely from exhaustion.\n\nBy late afternoon, the Turtle could already see the summit of the third mountain. He was close.\n\nOnly then did the Rabbit wake up. He looked down the mountain, assuming the Turtle was still trailing—but there was no trace of him. The Rabbit began sprinting as fast as he could.\n\nTo his surprise, he found the Turtle already resting at the top of the third mountain. The animals cheered and congratulated the Turtle. \n\nThe Rabbit felt deeply embarrassed. He apologized to the Turtle for mocking him. \n\nFrom that day on, the animals learned a valuable lesson: Never underestimate others.\n\n– THE END –";
        }
        else if (currentStatueStage == statueStage.HO_3)
        {
            storyString = "Bakit Mataas Ang Langit\n\nLong ago, the sky was very low, and there were no moon or stars. Have you ever wondered why the sky rose so high?\n\nMaria and her mother lived in a small nipa hut. Maria owned a golden comb and a necklace with shiny golden beads. \n\nEvery day, she would wear them and admire her reflection in the water, thinking, “Do I look beautiful today?” \n\nOne day, as Maria stared at her reflection, her mother called her.\n\n“Maria, pound the rice!” her mother said.\n\n“Yes, Mother,” Maria replied, but she didn’t move.\n\n“Hurry, Maria!” her mother shouted. “We have no rice to cook!”\n\n“Just a moment!” Maria answered, still staring at her reflection.\n\n“Maria, I’m telling you to pound some rice now. Hurry up,” the mother ordered angrily.\n\nMaria stood up and went straight to the mortar and pestle. She knew that when her mother got very angry, she had to obey quickly.\n\nBecause of this, she didn’t remove her comb and necklace and hurriedly started pounding rice. After a while, she began to sweat.\n\n“My necklace is getting soaked with sweat,” she muttered.\n\nShe took off her jewelry and hung it on the low sky, which she could easily reach. \n\nAs she worked, she glanced at her comb and necklace. “They’re so beautiful! I’ll wear them again soon,” she thought.\n\nTo finish faster, Maria lifted the pestle higher in order to slam it down harder. But each time she raised it, the pestle bumped the sky, pushing it upward. \n\nMaria didn’t notice until the sky was too high to reach, making it impossible for her to recover the comb and necklace.\n\nEven though she was sad, she looked at the brighter side of things. That night, Maria looked up and saw her comb glowing as the moon and her beads twinkling as stars. \n\n“My comb shines even brighter now,” she whispered, “and my necklace lights up the night!”\n\n– THE END –";
        }
        else if (currentStatueStage == statueStage.HO_4)
        {
            storyString = "Ang Kwento ng Dalawang Anghel\n\nTwo heavenly angels traveled the world. During their journey, they were caught by nightfall and searched for a place to stay, and found a grand house.\n\n“Let’s go there! Let’s ask if we can stay!” said the younger angel.\n\nKnock… knock… knock…\n\nThe door opened, and an old man appeared. His neck shimmered from the golden necklace he wore.\n\n“We were caught by night while traveling. May we stay here for the night…” said the older angel.\n\nThe old man didn’t reply right away and stared at them. He hesitated because they were disguised and he didn’t know they were angels. \n\nBut because they were polite, he let them in. He gave them a small, cramped room with a hard bed. \n\nHe didn’t offer them any food, even though he knew they were shivering from hunger. \n\nAs they were about to sleep, the older angel noticed a hole in the wall. He fixed it and sealed the hole. The younger angel just watched him. \n\nThe next morning, they thanked the old man and left, and continued on their journey.\n\n“I’m really hungry!” said the younger angel. \n\n“Okay, wait... over there is a small nipa hut... let’s check and see if someone can help us...” \n\nAs they approached the hut, they saw an elderly couple. It was clear from their posture that life was hard for them.\n\n“Good afternoon, did you lose your way?” asked the old man. \n\n“We were caught by nightfall, and we’re hungry… It’s embarrassing, but we’d like to ask if you have a bit of bread to spare so we can get through the night,” said the older angel. \n\n“Yes, we do. And it’s too late for you to travel again, so please stay the night!” said the old woman.\n\nEven though they were hungry, the younger angel noticed that the food they were given was the couple’s only food. \n\nHe offered it back, but the couple insisted they eat since they had a long journey the next day. \n\nMoreover, the couple gave up their bed for the angels and slept on the floor.\n\nThe next morning, the younger angel woke to loud crying. He went outside and saw the old woman crying while her husband tended to their only cow, which had died. \n\nThe younger angel, now angry, went back inside and confronted the older angel.\n\n“Why did you do that? That rich old man didn’t take care of us, but you fixed his wall. \n\nYet these kind old people gave us almost everything they had, and you let their cow die... Why!?” \n\n“I understand your anger, young angel… But when we were at that selfish man’s mansion, I saw treasure hidden behind a wall he seemed unaware of. Because of his wicked heart, I sealed it away.” \n\n“As for the elderly couple—last night, the angel of death came to take the old woman. But because they were kind to us… I gave him their cow instead.”\n\nIn life, there are many stories like this. We often judge too quickly. But what we see may not be what we think it is. We are blessed in ways we don’t always understand.\n\n– THE END –";
        }
        else if (currentStatueStage == statueStage.HO_5)
        {
            storyString = "Ang Kwento ng Tikbalang\n\n“Ate, is it true that Tikbalangs exist? Isn’t that just a way to scare kids into behaving?” Edith asked her eldest sister.\n\n“But Uncle Jose said they really do exist. He even said he was there when they caught one.”\n\n“Come on Ate, tell us what happened,” the youngest, Teresa, pleaded.\n\n“Alright, come here and listen.”\n\nThere was once a very beautiful young woman named Linda in Uncle Jose’s village. Many men courted her because she was not only beautiful but also kind.\n\nHowever, despite all the men in the village who admired her, none won her heart. \n\nOne day, she met a young man from Manila named Roberto. He was handsome, tall, and seemingly respectable. \n\nMany village girls were attracted to Roberto, but he only had eyes for Linda.\n\nThe young man's courtship was met with Linda’s love, and before long, they were married. Joyful days followed for the couple, who cherished each other deeply.\n\nTheir love was blessed with a beautiful baby girl named Ligaya. But dark clouds soon gathered over their peaceful life—Linda fell ill and eventually passed away. \n\nRoberto was left in deep sorrow, with only their infant daughter to comfort him.\n\nOne night, while Roberto was feeding the baby with a bottle, he sensed someone behind him. \n\nTo his shock, he saw Linda standing at the bedroom door—the same Linda who had just been buried the previous week. \n\nShe didn’t speak, but stretched out her arms as if asking for the baby.\n\n“No, Linda, you’re dead. This isn’t right!” Roberto said as he held the baby tighter and shook his head.\n\nThe woman left, but the next night she appeared again. Roberto refused even more firmly to give her the child. \n\nBut fear and confusion kept him from sleeping. When the same thing happened on the third night, he decided to consult the village elders.\n\n“That’s not Linda’s ghost, Roberto,” one elder said. \n\n“That’s a Tikbalang taking the form of your wife.” \n\n“There are Tikbalangs in our fields. They sleep in lumbang trees during the day and roam at night.”\n\n“Are Tikbalangs real? I thought they were just stories,” Roberto asked, bewildered. \n\n“They’re real. Do you know Karyo, the half-wit? He ended up like that because a Tikbalang took him when he was a child.” \n\n“He disappeared for two days and was found by his father under a lumbang tree.”\n\n“Oh no, what should I do? Each night it becomes more desperate to take the baby,” Roberto said, nearly in tears.\n\n“Don’t worry, we’ll prepare,” the elder promised. \n\nThat night, at the same hour, the Tikbalang came again, still in the form of Linda. \n\nJust as it was about to grab the baby from Roberto’s arms, men burst out of the room. \n\nStartled, the Tikbalang leapt out of the window. \n\nBut waiting outside were more men who grabbed its hair and tied it to a coconut tree. They beat it over and over until it cried out for mercy. \n\n“Forgive me! I’ll leave your place, just let me go. I’ll take all my followers with me. We’ll go far away and never bother you again. Please have mercy!” cried the Tikbalang. \n\nMoved by its cries and promise never to return, the village men released the creature.\n\nSince then, no Tikbalang has ever been seen again in the village. \n\nThe child, Ligaya, grew up to be a kind and beautiful woman like her mother. Even after marrying, she never left her father’s side and cared for him until his old age.\n\n“Your story was great, but scary, Ate,” said Edith. “We might not be able to sleep.”\n\n“Say your prayers before bed,” the eldest reminded with a teasing smile, “or the Tikbalang might pay you a visit!”\n\n– THE END –";
        }
        else if (currentStatueStage == statueStage.HO_6)
        {
            storyString = "The Monkey and the Turtle\n\nOne day, a Monkey met a Turtle on the road and asked, \"Where are you going?\"\n\n\"I am going to find something to eat, for I have had no food for three whole days,\" said the Turtle.\n\n\"I, too, am hungry,\" said the Monkey, \"and since we are both hungry, let us go together and hunt food for our stomach's sake.\"\n\nThey soon became good friends and chatted along the way, so that the time passed quickly. Before they had gone far, the Monkey saw a large bunch of yellow bananas on a tree at a distance.\n\n\"Oh, what a good sight that is!\" he cried. \"Don't you see the bananas hanging on that banana tree?” He pointed with his first finger toward the tree, “They are fine! I can taste them already.\"\n\nBut the Turtle was short-sighted and could not see them. After a while, they came near the tree, and then he finally saw them. The two friends were very glad. The mere sight of the ripe, yellow fruit made them hungrier.\n\nBut the Turtle could not climb the tree, so he agreed that the Monkey should go up alone and throw some of the fruit down to him.\n\nThe Monkey was up in a flash and, seating himself comfortably, he began to eat the ripest of the fruit, and forgot to drop any down to the Turtle waiting below.\n\nThe Turtle called for some, but the Monkey pretended not to hear. He ate even the peelings and refused to drop a bit to his friend, who was patiently begging under the tree.\n\nAt last, the Turtle became angry, very angry indeed. So, he thought he would take revenge.\n\nWhile the Monkey was having a good time filling his stomach, the Turtle gathered sharp, broken pieces of glass and stuck them, one by one, all around the banana tree. Then he hid himself under a coconut shell not far away.\n\nThe Monkey could not eat all the bananas, for there was enough to last a good-sized family for several days. He ate all that he could, and then he started coming down the tree with great difficulty.\n\nThe sharp glasses then cut the hand of the Monkey, wounding him. He had a hard time, and his hands were full of cuts in many places. The Turtle was satisfied with his revenge.\n\nBut now, the Monkey was very angry at the trick the Turtle had played on him. He began searching for the Turtle, planning to punish him. For a while, he could not find his former friend.\n\nTired and in pain, he sat down on a coconut shell nearby. His anger only grew stronger as he rested, thinking about what the Turtle had done.\n\nHe sat there for a long time, sore from his wounds and wondering where the Turtle could be. As he shifted his weight on the shell, the Turtle—who was hiding inside—made a small noise.\n\nThe Monkey heard it and looked around, confused. He couldn’t tell where the sound had come from. At last, he lifted the shell—and there was the Turtle!\n\n“Ah! So here you are!” the Monkey shouted. “Say your prayers. This is the end for you!”\n\nHe grabbed the Turtle by the neck and carried him to the riverbank. There, he prepared to punish him. He built a big fire and brought out a mortar and pestle. He was deciding whether to burn the Turtle or grind him into pieces.\n\nWhen everything was ready, he asked the Turtle to choose how he wanted to die: in the fire or in the mortar. The Turtle begged for his life. But when he saw that the Monkey would not forgive him, he cried, “Please, do anything—but don’t throw me into the water!”\n\nWhen the Monkey heard this, he thought it was the perfect punishment. He picked up the Turtle with his bleeding fingers and threw him as hard as he could into the river.\n\nTo the Monkey’s surprise, the Turtle was delighted. He laughed and popped his head out of the water.\n\n“This is my home!” the Turtle shouted. “The water is where I belong!”\n\nHearing this, the Monkey became so angry that he lost control. He jumped into the river to catch the Turtle—but he could not swim, and he drowned.\n\nSince that day, monkeys and turtles have been bitter enemies.\n\n- The End -";
        }
    }

